{"id":"top-100-sources","title":"Where the top 100 come from","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Which departments and job classes supply the 100 highest-paid employees, and how has that changed?","finding":"In fiscal 2013 the top 100 came mostly from Fire Department (51), Police (28) and Public Health (5); in fiscal 2026 from Police (58), Sheriff (16) and Public Health (11). The largest single source in fiscal 2026 is Sergeant 3 (class Q052) in Police: 24 of the 100, with overtime 54% of their pay. Over the 14 years, 91 pairings of department and class have supplied at least one of the 1,400 places; 27 supplied one in fiscal 2026.","how":"The 100 highest-paid people of each fiscal year, 2013 to 2026, as in the year-by-year table, counted by the department and job class of their main row. “Places” are counted once a year, so a person in the top 100 for five years counts five times. Shares are of the fiscal 2026 pay of the people counted. Titles are printed as the payroll prints them (Dep is deputy, Chf is chief, Asst is assistant, Sprv is supervisor, Ofc is officer, EMT is emergency medical technician).","limits":"The shift toward police and sheriff’s classes follows overtime, and overtime usually follows vacancies: a department short of staff pays the people it has to cover the shifts. Rank-and-file classes on the list are there for hours worked, not for their rate of pay. Physicians and the Retirement System’s investment staff are on it for salary and for pay recorded as “other”, which their terms of employment set.","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"job","t":"Job class","type":"text"},{"k":"placesLatest","t":"In the top 100, fiscal 2026","type":"int"},{"k":"placesFirst","t":"In the top 100, fiscal 2013","type":"int"},{"k":"placesAll","t":"Places, 2013 to 2026","type":"int"},{"k":"years","t":"Years with at least one","type":"int"},{"k":"averageLatest","t":"Their average pay, fiscal 2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"overtimeShare","t":"Of which o